body:
Hi folks,

I'm one of the maintainers for TCG Plugins in QEMU upstream (since April 2026) and additional languages support has been on my list for a long time.
A few months ago, I made some changes to allow C++ plugins to directly include the QEMU plugins headers.
Also, Rust has been on my mind, and I took a look at `qemu-rs` already.

One design issue that was left IMHO, was that existing plugins interface couldn't hold fully the plugin state, and thus we needed to use some form of global variable, or accept leaking memory.
Thus, I sent a [series](https://lore.kernel.org/qemu-devel/20260616184230.303038-1-pierrick.bouvier@oss.qualcomm.com/#t) to make sure all callbacks/API functions now have a `void *userdata`, which is now merged.

As a result, we can now have a proper C++ plugin without any global variable nor memory leak. See this [commit](https://gitlab.com/qemu-project/qemu/-/commit/76b70dcb8f425363cf4c767cd84761e219349bdd).
This pattern works with Rust also, and removes the need to allocate memory per callback.

```
 QEMU_PLUGIN_EXPORT int qemu_plugin_install(qemu_plugin_id_t id,
                                            const qemu_info_t *info,
                                            int argc, char **argv)
 {
    Plugin *plugin = new Plugin;

    qemu_plugin_register_vcpu_tb_trans_cb(
        id,
        [](struct qemu_plugin_tb *tb, void *userdata) {
            Plugin *p = static_cast<Plugin*>(userdata);
            p->tb_trans(tb);
        },
        plugin);

    qemu_plugin_register_atexit_cb(
        id,
        [](void *userdata) {
            Plugin *p = static_cast<Plugin*>(userdata);
            p->at_exit();
            delete p;
        },
        plugin);

     return 0;
 }
```

So now we have this, and Rust support upstream, I would be really happy to see native support for Rust coming.

I wanted to see how people around here feel about it, and if someone would be happy to be involved.
I would totally understand if people wants to keep qemu-rs downstream, nothing wrong with it.

Coming back to TCG plugins, for QEMU plugins, we can provide support upstream, with both approaches:
- bindgen based API (similar to C++ code above)
- native Rust API, inspired from qemu-rs design.

The only important things I would like make sure is that we don't:
- pay for the abstraction of native Rust API
- leak memory
- use global variables

What do you think?

https://github.com/novafacing/qemu-rs/blob/ed66662b21e0a217eb240543415b6462689e7a69/qemu-plugin/src/lib.rs#L128 

Now I come to try this again (via the `disas()` function), it seems it doesn't work - I get segfaults. I thought I had seen it work previously. Maybe I was wrong. Judging from gdb backtraces it looks like there's several `RtlCreateHeap` calls, and I guess we must be trying to free in the wrong heap, or something. It's dying inside a `RtlFreeHeap` call.

Anyway, the magic we need to get the right `g_free` is:
1. Locate the `libglib-2.0-0.dll` DLL in memory.
2. in that, locate the `g_free` function.
3. Call that.

I have this working in principle:
```rust
#[cfg(windows)]
unsafe fn g_free(mem: *mut c_void) {

    use libloading::os::windows::Symbol;
    if !mem.is_null() {
        unsafe {
            let lib = libloading::os::windows::Library::open_already_loaded("libglib-2.0-0.dll").expect("libglib-2.0-0.dll should already be loaded");
            let g_free : Symbol<unsafe extern fn(*mut c_void)> = lib.get(b"g_free").expect("find g_free");
            g_free(mem);
        }
    }
}
```

(using the libloading crate which hides a lot of the windows API nastiness)

which works, but of course it does the lookup on every single call to `g_free` which is no good. I'll keep on this next week and do a lazy_static or something, but just wanted to give you heads up I'm working on something.
